assertion  XSH.fdim.001
anchor     functions/fdim.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fdim.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       fdim(5, 3), (5.5, 0.5), (-1, -3) and (0.5, -0.5) give 2, 5, 2 and 1.
test       t/001-greater.c

assertion  XSH.fdim.002
anchor     functions/fdim.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fdim.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       fdim(3, 5), (3, 3), (-2, -2), (-5, 2), (+0, -0) and (-0, +0) give +0
           (positive zero).
test       t/002-not-greater.c

assertion  XSH.fdim.003
anchor     functions/fdim.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fdim.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       fdim(NaN, 1), fdim(1, NaN) and fdim(NaN, NaN) return a NaN.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/003-nan.c

assertion  XSH.fdim.004
anchor     functions/fdim.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fdim.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/fdim.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      2001
says       fdim(MAX, -MAX), with MAX the largest finite value of the type,
           overflows: the result is HUGE_VAL (HUGE_VALF, HUGE_VALL) and the range
           error is reported through errno (ERANGE) and the overflow exception as
           math_errhandling selects.
test       t/004-overflow.c

assertion  XSH.fdim.005
anchor     functions/fdim.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fdim.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       fdim(1.5*MIN, MIN), with MIN the smallest normal value of the type,
           returns MIN/2 exactly when subnormal numbers exist.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard). A range error may accompany the result; it is not judged.
test       t/005-subnormal.c

assertion  XSH.fdim.006
anchor     functions/fdim.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fdim.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.23
class      required
since      2001
says       With errno holding EDOM before the call, fdim(5, 3) leaves it
           unchanged and, when exception flags are in use, raises none of the
           invalid, divide-by-zero and overflow exceptions.
test       t/006-no-error.c
